# Local experimental oxpec module

`oxpec.upstream.c` retains the source and all notices fetched from the public
[Jovian mirror's 7.2.4-valve1 tag](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Jovian-Experiments/linux/7.2.4-valve1/drivers/platform/x86/oxpec.c).
This is a named Valve-tag mirror, not an authenticated retrieval of the official
Valve source archive. Its exact official equivalence remains unconfirmed.

`oxpec.c` contains two local changes: the seven-line ONEXPLAYER 3 board DMI entry described in the
[reviewed upstream patch](https://lists.openwall.net/linux-kernel/2026/09/20/1381).
`onexplayer3-dmi.patch` records that addition. `oxpec-acpi-lock-release.patch`
records a separate fix to the original EC read helper: after an `ec_read()`
failure, it attempts to release the acquired ACPI global lock before returning.
Successful reads retain their original behavior. Vendor/GPL notices remain intact.
Charging registers, fan/turbo policy and teardown behavior are unchanged.
This module has no automatic restoration of hardware settings modified
through its writable interfaces; see [the build-path trace](../BUILD_PATH.md).

The exact official `linux-neptune-72-headers-7.2.4.valve1-1` package is staged in
`research/cache/oxpec-headers/`, with its prepared build tree extracted beneath
`unpacked/`. Nothing was installed into host `/usr` or a device. No package manager,
integrity verification, firmware operation or module loading is part of this build.

Build from any working directory:

```sh
sh /root/steamosmod/research/charging/module/build.sh
# A caller can select an already available compatible compiler:
sh /root/steamosmod/research/charging/module/build.sh CC=/path/to/gcc
```

The build requires tools compatible with the exact headers' configuration and
compiler flags. GCC 16.1.1 was used for the stock kernel. `build.log` records the
initial failed host-only attempt. A locally staged Ubuntu toolchain now produces
the experimental `oxpec.ko` successfully:

```sh
python3 /root/steamosmod/research/charging/module/stage_toolchain.py
python3 /root/steamosmod/research/charging/module/build_staged.py
```

`stage_toolchain.py` downloads fixed GCC16 Noble PPA packages and small official
Ubuntu helpers to `research/cache/oxpec-toolchain/` and uses `dpkg-deb -x` only;
it executes no package installer. `build_staged.py` selects those tools and scopes
their helper library path to one child build process tree. No libc/loader is
staged or exported globally. Stock headers/configuration remain unchanged.

[BUILD_STATUS.md](BUILD_STATUS.md), `build-staged-lock-fix.log`, `staged-runtime.log` and
`candidate-metadata.txt` describe the successful build and its bounds. The
compiler is an experimental GCC16 March snapshot, different from the stock June
compiler, and source equivalence with official Valve source remains unconfirmed.
The earlier DMI-only candidate and metadata are preserved as
`oxpec.before-acpi-lock-fix.ko` and `candidate-metadata.before-acpi-lock-fix.txt`;
`build-staged.log` records that earlier build.
Compilation is not target loading or charging acceptance. No deployment command
is supplied here.
